The code statements that lead to the bug are:
wiphy_delayed_work_cancel(mld->wiphy, &mld_vif->emlsr.prevent_done_wk);
wiphy_delayed_work_cancel(mld->wiphy, &mld_vif->emlsr.tmp_non_bss_done_wk);
wiphy_work_cancel(mld->wiphy, &mld_vif->emlsr.unblock_tpt_wk);
wiphy_delayed_work_cancel(mld->wiphy, &mld_vif->emlsr.check_tpt_wk);
Reason:
The bug is that while other similar delayed works are cancelled when the station state moves down (from authorized to assoc), the delayed work `mlo_scan_start_wk` is missing from this cancellation list. This omission allows `mlo_scan_start_wk` to remain queued after disconnection, potentially executing after the vif is freed (use‑after‑free) or interfering with interface changes (init‑after‑queue).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
wiphy_delayed_work_cancel(mld->wiphy, &mld_vif->mlo_scan_start_wk);
Reason:
This addition cancels the delayed work `mlo_scan_start_wk` during the disconnection path (when moving from `IEEE80211_STA_AUTHORIZED` to `IEEE80211_STA_ASSOC`). By properly cleaning up the work, it prevents any delayed execution after the vif becomes invalid, thereby avoiding both use‑after‑free and init‑after‑queue issues.

